{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1396398-how-do-mages-experience-fate-buffs-to-their-magic","id":1396398,"title":"How do mages experience fate buffs to their magic?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-06-14T09:02:01-07:00","id":1396398,"title":"How do mages experience fate buffs to their magic?","content":"Say a mage is operating under Fate 2 Fools Rush In with the +3 reach effect that allows the spell to add to spellcasting pools ... how does that actually manifest? In a narrative sense I mean. What does the mage actually in-story get out of that kind of spell?<br /> <br /> At times I've been tempted to describe it as just happening to run across the absolutely perfect yantra for the specific spell being cast, one that by its very nature is so overly-specific and/or fleeting that it won't apply to any subsequent attempt at spellcasting. Like bird crap falling from the sky such that its motions trace out a Supernal Symbol in the air at the precise time and place that the mage was going to need to imagine that Symbol in the Imago.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3642-lareath","name":"Lareath","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"186"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-16T17:56:57-07:00","id":1396976,"title":"","content":"I think the benefit to Fate is that with so much of it being probability manipulation, the sky is the limit to how to describe how something works out. This is true for both positive and negative probability manipulation. You're example is pretty cool. And there are many, many more that can work. One of the simplest ways for hexes to work out is distraction: the person trips, someone calls their name and they turn their head, they stub their toe, a bug flies in their mouth, etc.<br /> <br /> In a positive sense, one way for it to work is if the spellcaster has their mage sight up then they can literally see the symbols forming in the air that they need to use (presuming the benefit is going to spellcasting). If the benefit is for skills then the ST can draw on the idea of Flow, that someone is &quot;in the moment, on fire, really focused,&quot; etc. If the action taken is mental, then you can say that an idea pops into the person's head. If it's physical, flow works really well. If it's social they might have a phrase or set of words that pop into their head, or maybe they get a feeling for the mood in the room or the attitude someone else has.<br /> <br /> Honestly, the sky is the limit for description.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7825-falcon777","name":"Falcon777","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}